{"source_key":"sha256-db92dd9d54884ccb80de5ab4e2691d5a","record_index":341,"record_hash":"sha256:0c7a08b489b8044aafff5f7f35954bc3632f8628a1f03da45aba3b8ce16b19cc","timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:30:54.115Z","kind":"tool_call","source_event_kind":"codex:response_item:custom_tool_call","role":null,"text":null,"tool_name":"exec","tool_kind":"other","tool_input":"const patch = \"*** Begin Patch\\n*** Add File: [REDACTED:path]# ProjectMeta as the Single Project-Metadata Source\\n+\\n+Status: Draft for review\\n+\\n+## Summary\\n+\\n+Remove the project target fields from the live `virtual_branches.toml` in one\\n+release and make repository-local Git config, represented by `ProjectMeta`, the\\n+only live source of truth.\\n+\\n+Oplog snapshots retain backwards compatibility without retaining a live mirror:\\n+each new snapshot stores an authoritative `project_meta.toml` blob and a\\n+snapshot-only legacy `[default_target]` projection inside its copy of\\n+`virtual_branches.toml`. Current restore code reads the authoritative blob and\\n+strips the compatibility projection before writing live metadata.\\n+\\n+This deliberately does not promise fully safe continued oplog use after\\n+downgrading to an already-shipped release. ProjectMeta-aware older releases can\\n+open the project and restore snapshots created by the new release, but may\\n+create target-free snapshots after the downgrade that their own restore code\\n+cannot handle correctly. Pre-ProjectMeta releases are unsupported.\\n+\\n+## Goals\\n+\\n+- Make `ProjectMeta` the sole live owner of:\\n+  - the target ref;\\n+  - the stable target commit id;\\n+  - the push remote.\\n+- Stop serializing `[default_target]` into live `virtual_branches.toml`.\\n+- Stop writing project metadata through workspace/ref-metadata adapters.\\n+- Preserve restoration of existing oplog snapshots.\\n+- Make new snapshots readable by ProjectMeta-aware older releases without\\n+  modifying live TOML.\\n+- Preserve direct upgrades for projects whose metadata has not yet been ported.\\n+- Preserve cross-process workspace refresh when ProjectMeta changes.\\n+- Perform the cut in one release, without a bridge release or a permanent\\n+  compatibility mirror.\\n+\\n+## Non-goals\\n+\\n+- Fully support continued undo/redo after downgrading to an already-shipped\\n+  release.\\n+- Support downgrading to a pre-ProjectMeta release.\\n+- Store or restore the repository's complete Git configuration.\\n+- Change the oplog snapshot version.\\n+- Remove the existing nullable `default_target_*` SQLite columns in this change.\\n+- Remove the legacy `Target` type where it is still used for per-stack\\n+  `branch_targets` or historical snapshot decoding.\\n+- Add a downgrade-preparation command or updater hook.\\n+\\n+## Current state\\n+\\n+`ProjectMeta` stores `target_ref`, `target_commit_id`, and `push_remote` under\\n+the repository-common local Git config. The `gitbutler.project.portedMeta`\\n+marker selects Git config over the legacy TOML fallback.\\n+\\n+Project metadata is nevertheless still mirrored into the workspace metadata\\n+shape. `ProjectMeta::persist()` writes Git config and then backfills the\\n+workspace, whose TOML adapter maps the values into `[default_target]`. Several\\n+materialization paths also call `Workspace::set_project_meta()` solely to keep\\n+that mirror current.\\n+\\n+The migration is not guaranteed complete by elapsed time. A ProjectMeta read is\\n+currently side-effect-free; an unported project is only ported on a subsequent\\n+metadata write. Dormant and read-only projects may therefore still have their\\n+only target in `virtual_branches.toml`.\\n+\\n+The oplog has no independent ProjectMeta record. Snapshot creation stores the\\n+live `virtual_branches.toml` wholesale, while restore writes that file back and\\n+copies its target into Git config. Removing `[default_target]` without changing\\n+this flow would make target changes non-restorable and could erase valid Git\\n+config during restore.\\n+\\n+## Ownership after the change\\n+\\n+| Data | Live owner | Oplog owner | Compatibility representation |\\n+| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n+| Target ref | `ProjectMeta` in local Git config | `project_meta.toml` | Snapshot-only legacy target |\\n+| Stable target commit | `ProjectMeta` in local Git config | `project_meta.toml` | Snapshot-only legacy target |\\n+| Push remote | `ProjectMeta` in local Git config | `project_meta.toml` | Snapshot-only legacy target |\\n+| Stack and branch metadata | Virtual-branches storage | `virtual_branches.toml` | Existing format |\\n+| Per-stack branch targets | Virtual-branches storage | `virtual_branches.toml` | Existing format |\\n+\\n+The snapshot-only legacy target is an encoding adapter, not a source of truth.\\n+It is synthesized from the snapshot's ProjectMeta and is never written to the\\n+live file by current code.\\n+\\n+## Live ProjectMeta behavior\\n+\\n+### Reads\\n+\\n+For a ported repository, `Context::project_meta()` reads only the\\n+repository-local Git config.\\n+\\n+For an unported repository, the first ProjectMeta load performs a one-time\\n+migration:\\n+\\n+1. Parse only the legacy `[default_target]` fields needed to construct\\n+   `ProjectMeta`.\\n+2. Apply the existing migration repair rules:\\n+   - preserve an existing stable target commit;\\n+   - fill a missing commit from the target ref when possible;\\n+   - clear a target ref that no longer exists.\\n+3. Persist ProjectMeta and `portedMeta=true` to local Git config.\\n+4. Mark the live virtual-branches metadata for normalization without the legacy\\n+   target.\\n+\\n+The narrow legacy parser is input-only. Once the ported marker is written, live\\n+TOML never participates in ProjectMeta resolution again.\\n+\\n+This fallback protects direct upgrades that skipped the earlier migration\\n+release. It can be removed later based on an explicit compatibility decision,\\n+not merely elapsed time.\\n+\\n+### Writes\\n+\\n+All ProjectMeta changes go through one config persistence path. A successful\\n+change:\\n+\\n+1. writes or removes the three ProjectMeta keys;\\n+2. retains `gitbutler.project.portedMeta=true`;\\n+3. best-effort writes the existing GitButler refresh sentinel when config\\n+   actually changed;\\n+4. invalidates the in-process workspace cache at the Context boundary.\\n+\\n+It does not open virtual-branches metadata and does not modify workspace\\n+metadata.\\n+\\n+The refresh notification must move with the source of truth. Today ProjectMeta\\n+writes trigger it indirectly through the TOML storage choke point. Without this\\n+change, target updates made by the CLI or another application process would no\\n+longer refresh the desktop workspace view.\\n+\\n+## Live TOML normalization\\n+\\n+The live virtual-branches serialization shape no longer contains\\n+`default_target`. Deserialization remains tolerant of the unknown historical\\n+section so an old file or a file written by a downgraded binary can still be\\n+opened.\\n+\\n+The next current-version metadata write serializes the canonical target-free\\n+shape. The one-time ProjectMeta migration must ensure the target is in Git\\n+config before normalization can discard it.\\n+\\n+If an older ProjectMeta-aware binary is run and reintroduces `[default_target]`,\\n+the current version ignores it as an authority and removes it on the next\\n+normalization. There is no field-by-field reconciliation and no conflict\\n+resolution between the two stores.\\n+\\n+The existing SQLite target columns remain present but are not a source for\\n+ProjectMeta. They should be left nullable and unused so the change does not\\n+introduce an unrelated database compatibility boundary.\\n+\\n+## Oplog snapshot format\\n+\\n+New snapshots retain the existing version-3 tree and add one optional root\\n+blob:\\n+\\n+```text\\n+.\\n+├── conflicts/…\\n+├── index/…\\n+├── project_meta.toml\\n+├── target_tree/…\\n+├── virtual_branches/…\\n+├── virtual_branches.toml\\n+└── worktree/…\\n+```\\n+\\n+`project_meta.toml` contains only the ProjectMeta values:\\n+\\n+```toml\\n+targetRef = \\\"refs/remotes/origin/main\\\"\\n+targetCommitId = \\\"[REDACTED:entropy]\\\"\\n+pushRemote = \\\"origin\\\"\\n+```\\n+\\n+Optional values are omitted. Parsing ignores unknown fields so compatible\\n+additions do not require a new filename. A future breaking encoding should use\\n+a new filename and a feature-detected reader rather than overloading this one.\\n+\\n+The oplog owns a small storage DTO with string fields and explicit conversion to\\n+and from the core domain type. The core `ProjectMeta` type does not gain serde\\n+semantics solely for the oplog.\\n+\\n+### Snapshot creation\\n+\\n+Snapshot preparation resolves ProjectMeta once and reuses that value for:\\n+\\n+- target-tree lookup;\\n+- commit traversal boundaries;\\n+- authoritative `project_meta.toml` serialization;\\n+- the snapshot-only legacy target projection.\\n+\\n+Before storing the snapshot's `virtual_branches.toml`, the oplog adds a legacy\\n+`[default_target]` representation to an in-memory compatibility copy. It does\\n+not write this copy to disk or import it into the live database.\\n+\\n+The old target shape requires `remoteUrl`, which ProjectMeta intentionally does\\n+not persist. The compatibility encoder derives it from the currently configured\\n+remote and falls back to an empty string if lookup fails. The encoder only emits\\n+a legacy target when both the target ref and a non-null target commit exist,\\n+because the old format cannot safely represent partial target state.\\n+\\n+The snapshot keeps `virtual_branches.toml` because stack state still belongs\\n+there and existing snapshot enumeration uses that entry to recognize oplog\\n+commits.\\n+\\n+### Snapshot version\\n+\\n+Snapshots continue to emit version 3. The new blob is an additive,\\n+feature-detected entry.\\n+\\n+Before a future version bump, readers should be changed separately so the\\n+direct `worktree` layout is recognized for supported versions greater than or\\n+equal to 3. This design does not depend on that cleanup.\\n+\\n+## Oplog restore\\n+\\n+Restore parses and validates all required snapshot metadata before mutating the\\n+worktree, refs, live metadata, database, or Git config.\\n+\\n+ProjectMeta selection is:\\n+\\n+1. Use `project_meta.toml` when present.\\n+2. Otherwise, derive ProjectMeta from `[default_target]` in the historical\\n+   snapshot's `virtual_branches.toml`.\\n+3. Fail before mutation if the selected representation is malformed or lacks\\n+   target information required by the snapshot operation.\\n+\\n+After selection, restore:\\n+\\n+1. reconstitutes stored commits and workspace state as it does today;\\n+2. removes the compatibility `[default_target]` from the snapshot TOML;\\n+3. writes/imports only the target-free stack and branch metadata as live state;\\n+4. restores branch refs;\\n+5. persists the selected ProjectMeta directly to Git config;\\n+6. restores the worktree and index according to the existing version-3 flow;\\n+7. invalidates the workspace cache and emits the existing refresh notification.\\n+\\n+The current `resync_project_meta_from_legacy()` direction is removed. Historical\\n+TOML remains a decoding fallback for old snapshots, but restored live TOML is\\n+never authoritative for current ProjectMeta.\\n+\\n+Code that reconstructs a historical applied-branches tree should use the\\n+snapshot's selected ProjectMeta rather than the current project's target. This\\n+avoids mixing present target state into interpretation of an older snapshot.\\n+\\n+## Downgrade contract\\n+\\n+The supported behavior is intentionally limited:\\n+\\n+- Current and future binaries fully support the target-free live format.\\n+- Current binaries restore both old snapshots and new snapshots.\\n+- ProjectMeta-aware older binaries can open a ported project because\\n+  `portedMeta=true` continues to route reads to Git config.\\n+- Those older binaries can restore snapshots created by the new version because\\n+  the snapshot TOML contains the synthesized legacy target.\\n+- Continued oplog use after downgrade is not guaranteed: an older binary may\\n+  create a snapshot from the target-free live TOML that its own restore path\\n+  later mishandles.\\n+- Pre-ProjectMeta binaries are unsupported.\\n+\\n+This contract removes live split brain without adding a bridge release,\\n+permanent mirror, compatibility command, or updater-specific state transition.\\n+\\n+## Failure handling\\n+\\n+- A malformed authoritative snapshot blob fails restore before repository\\n+  mutation; it does not silently fall back to legacy data.\\n+- Absence of the authoritative blob means an old snapshot and enables the\\n+  legacy fallback.\\n+- Failure to derive `remoteUrl` does not fail snapshot creation; the legacy-only\\n+  value becomes empty.\\n+- Failure to persist ProjectMeta during restore prevents completion and must not\\n+  leave the workspace cache presenting the restored state with the old target.\\n+- Refresh-sentinel failure remains best-effort and is logged without failing the\\n+  metadata mutation.\\n+- An unported project with no valid legacy target follows the existing\\n+  uninitialized/bootstrap behavior; migration must not invent a target beyond\\n+  the existing repair and bootstrap rules.\\n+\\n+## Test strategy\\n+\\n+### ProjectMeta and migration\\n+\\n+- An unported project with only `[default_target]` is ported on first\\n+  ProjectMeta load.\\n+- The one-time migration preserves an existing stable target commit.\\n+- A missing target commit is repaired from the target ref when possible.\\n+- A direct upgrade normalizes live TOML only after config persistence succeeds.\\n+- A ported repository never reads a conflicting live legacy target.\\n+- ProjectMeta writes no longer write target values to TOML or workspace\\n+  metadata.\\n+- A second Context observes the config change and the refresh sentinel is\\n+  emitted only when config changes.\\n+\\n+### Live virtual-branches storage\\n+\\n+- Historical TOML containing `[default_target]` remains readable.\\n+- Canonical serialization omits `[default_target]`.\\n+- Reopening and writing a file created by an older binary removes the legacy\\n+  section without changing stack or branch metadata.\\n+- Existing per-stack `branch_targets` continue to round-trip.\\n+\\n+### Oplog\\n+\\n+- A new snapshot contains authoritative `project_meta.toml`.\\n+- The same snapshot contains a legacy target in its compatibility TOML, while\\n+  the live TOML remains target-free.\\n+- New snapshot restore round-trips target ref, stable commit, and push remote.\\n+- Old snapshot restore derives ProjectMeta from its legacy target.\\n+- Both restore paths leave live TOML target-free.\\n+- Undoing a target-ref change restores the old ProjectMeta exactly.\\n+- Undoing a target-commit-only update restores the old stable commit.\\n+- Snapshot tree reconstruction uses the snapshot target rather than the current\\n+  target.\\n+- Snapshot enumeration still recognizes new snapshots.\\n+- Version-3 worktree and untracked-file restoration is unchanged.\\n+- Malformed `project_meta.toml` fails before worktree, ref, config, TOML, or DB\\n+  mutation.\\n+- Missing `project_meta.toml` alone selects the old-snapshot fallback.\\n+\\n+### Compatibility\\n+\\n+- The compatibility TOML parses with the legacy representation expected by a\\n+  ProjectMeta-aware older release.\\n+- Restoring that compatibility TOML through the older restore semantics does\\n+  not clear ProjectMeta config.\\n+- The project database retains the columns expected by older binaries.\\n+\\n+## Expected code boundaries\\n+\\n+- `but-core`: direct ProjectMeta config parsing, repair, persistence, and refresh\\n+  notification.\\n+- `but-ctx`: one-time migration orchestration, cache invalidation, and removal of\\n+  legacy dual-write helpers.\\n+- `but-meta`: target-free live serialization plus input-only historical parsing;\\n+  existing stack and branch persistence remains unchanged.\\n+- `gitbutler-oplog`: authoritative snapshot DTO, legacy compatibility encoding,\\n+  feature-detected restore, and restore preflight.\\n+- Existing target mutation and materialization callers: delete legacy\\n+  `Workspace::set_project_meta()` and `set_default_target()` plumbing in favor of\\n+  the centralized ProjectMeta path.\\n+\\n+No new crate, database table, background migration service, feature flag, or\\n+frontend setting is required.\\n*** End Patch\";\ntext(await tools.apply_patch(patch));","source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T13:30:54.115Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","status":"completed","call_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","name":"exec","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"}}}}